    {
      "page_title": "2. Indexing",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. What it is",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "**What it is:** a separate ==sorted structure==, usually a B-tree",
            "It maps column values to row locations",
            "Lookups skip the full table scan"
          ],
          "style": "dot",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Why it exists",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "**Why it exists:** scanning 10M rows is ==O(n)==",
            "A B-tree lookup is ==O(log n)==",
            "That is only about 3-4 page reads"
          ],
          "style": "dot",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Used for",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "**Used for:** `WHERE` filters",
            "`JOIN` keys and `ORDER BY`",
            "Enforcing `UNIQUE` on email or user_id"
          ],
          "style": "star",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. HOW IT WORKS",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Table `users(id, email, created_at)` holds 10M rows. Same query, before and after an index.",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "pink"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Step",
            "Plan",
            "Time"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "No index",
              "Seq Scan",
              "~800 ms"
            ],
            [
              "CREATE INDEX idx_users_email",
              "Index Scan",
              "~0.1 ms"
            ],
            [
              "Index path",
              "root, internal, leaf, heap row",
              "3-4 reads"
            ]
          ],
          "highlightRow": 1,
          "frame": "dashed"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "SELECT * FROM users",
            "WHERE email = 'a@x.com';",
            "CREATE INDEX idx_users_email",
            "  ON users(email);"
          ],
          "note": "same query, plan changes"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Composite `(user_id, created_at)` serves `WHERE user_id = 7 ORDER BY created_at` by the __leftmost-prefix__ rule.",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Benefits and trade-offs",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Reads drop from O(n) to ==O(log n)==",
            "Same index makes sorts and range scans cheap",
            "Covering index: `CREATE INDEX ... INCLUDE (name)`",
            "Answers from the index, **no** table lookup"
          ],
          "style": "check",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Every INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE updates every index",
            "Write cost and storage grow per index",
            "Boolean (low cardinality) columns skip the index",
            "`WHERE lower(email) = ...` needs an index on that expression"
          ],
          "style": "cross",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "INTERVIEW TAKEAWAY",
          "text": "Index columns in WHERE, JOIN and ORDER BY; confirm with EXPLAIN.\nAvoid over-indexing write-heavy tables."
        }
      ],
      "hero": "postgresql",
      "subtitle": "skip the full scan"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "2. Transactions",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "**What it is:** a group of statements treated as ==one logical unit== that fully succeeds (`COMMIT`) or is fully undone (`ROLLBACK`).",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "**Why it exists:** a crash or error halfway through a multi-step change would leave data ==half-updated==.",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "**Used for:** money transfers, order placement plus stock decrement, and any change touching __more than one row or table__.",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "HOW IT WORKS",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "BEGIN;",
            "UPDATE accounts SET bal=bal-100",
            "  WHERE id = 1;",
            "UPDATE accounts SET bal=bal+100",
            "  WHERE id = 2;",
            "COMMIT;"
          ],
          "note": "both UPDATEs land together or not at all"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "tables",
          "spec": {
            "tables": [
              {
                "title": "Before",
                "headers": [
                  "id",
                  "bal"
                ],
                "rows": [
                  [
                    "1",
                    "500"
                  ],
                  [
                    "2",
                    "300"
                  ]
                ],
                "caption": "total 800"
              },
              {
                "title": "After COMMIT",
                "headers": [
                  "id",
                  "bal"
                ],
                "rows": [
                  [
                    "1",
                    "400"
                  ],
                  [
                    "2",
                    "400"
                  ]
                ],
                "caption": "total 800"
              }
            ],
            "arrow": true
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "If the 2nd UPDATE fails, `ROLLBACK` restores account 1 to ==500==",
            "So no money vanishes: the total stays 800",
            "**Savepoints** undo only part: `SAVEPOINT s1;` then `ROLLBACK TO s1;`"
          ],
          "style": "dot",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "BEGIN;",
            "SAVEPOINT s1;",
            "-- risky step fails here",
            "ROLLBACK TO s1; -- keeps earlier work",
            "COMMIT;"
          ],
          "note": "undoes only work after the savepoint"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Benefits",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "The app never sees a ==half-finished state==",
            "Error handling is simpler: one `ROLLBACK` undoes everything"
          ],
          "style": "check",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Things to consider",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Long transactions hold **locks** and bloat logs: keep them short, never wait on user input inside one",
            "A transaction spans __one database__; across services you need ==sagas== or two-phase commit"
          ],
          "style": "cross",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "INTERVIEW TAKEAWAY",
          "text": "Explain the 100-unit transfer and say 'all or nothing': both UPDATEs commit, or ROLLBACK undoes both.\nThen add that you keep transactions short, with no user input inside."
        }
      ],
      "hero": "postgresql",
      "subtitle": "All or nothing"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "3. ACID",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. ACID",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "**What it is:** four guarantees of a reliable transaction: ==Atomicity==, ==Consistency==, ==Isolation==, ==Durability==.",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "**Why it exists:** predictable behavior despite crashes, power loss and concurrent users.",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "**Used for:** banking, payments, inventory and bookings, where wrong data costs real money.",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "HOW IT WORKS",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Letter",
            "Guarantee",
            "Mechanism"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "**A**tomicity",
              "All or nothing",
              "Undo log"
            ],
            [
              "**C**onsistency",
              "Valid state to valid state",
              "`CHECK bal >= 0`, FKs"
            ],
            [
              "**I**solation",
              "Hides partial work",
              "Tunable strength"
            ],
            [
              "**D**urability",
              "COMMIT survives crash",
              "WAL fsynced first"
            ]
          ],
          "frame": "dashed"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "**Example:** `INSERT` of a 100 USD order returns OK, then the server loses power. After restart, ==WAL replay== brings the order back.",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "pink"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "BEGIN;",
            "INSERT INTO orders(id, amt)",
            "  VALUES (1, 100);",
            "COMMIT; -- WAL fsynced, then OK"
          ],
          "note": "Power cut after COMMIT: row survives"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Benefit: predictable, correct data under failure",
            "Benefit: correct data under concurrency too"
          ],
          "style": "check",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Consider: ACID costs throughput",
            "Many NoSQL stores relax it for speed and scale (BASE)"
          ],
          "style": "cross",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "INTERVIEW TAKEAWAY",
          "text": "Give one sentence and one failure scenario per letter. Note: 'C' in ACID is not the 'C' in CAP."
        }
      ],
      "hero": "postgresql",
      "subtitle": "Four guarantees"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "4. Locking",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "**What it is:** a transaction holds a ==claim== on a row or table, so others must wait or fail."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "**Why it exists:** two transactions updating one row at once cause a __lost update__."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "**Used for:** protecting counters, balances and inventory under concurrent writes."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "HOW IT WORKS",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "{green:Shared (read)} locks: many readers at once",
            "{red:Exclusive (write)} lock: one writer, all others blocked"
          ],
          "style": "dot"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Step",
            "T1 (buyer A)",
            "T2 (buyer B)"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "1",
              "FOR UPDATE locks row 5",
              "sends FOR UPDATE"
            ],
            [
              "2",
              "stock = 1, sells it, sets 0",
              "**waits** at its lock"
            ],
            [
              "3",
              "COMMIT, lock released",
              "lock granted"
            ],
            [
              "4",
              "done",
              "sees stock = 0, __refuses sale__"
            ]
          ],
          "highlightRow": 3,
          "frame": "dashed"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "BEGIN;",
            "SELECT stock FROM tickets",
            "  WHERE id = 5 FOR UPDATE;",
            "UPDATE tickets SET stock = 0",
            "  WHERE id = 5;",
            "COMMIT;"
          ],
          "note": "row lock held until COMMIT"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "-- optimistic: version column",
            "UPDATE tickets",
            "  SET qty = 4, ver = 8",
            "  WHERE id = 5 AND ver = 7;",
            "-- 0 rows changed? re-read, retry"
          ],
          "note": "no lock; retry on 0 rows"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Benefits: correct results on hot rows, no extra infrastructure",
            "Optimistic locking: high throughput when conflicts are rare"
          ],
          "style": "check",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Things to consider: ==deadlock==, T1 waits on T2 and T2 on T1; DB aborts one victim",
            "Take locks in a consistent order and retry",
            "Long-held locks serialize traffic; table locks block all writers"
          ],
          "style": "cross",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "INTERVIEW TAKEAWAY",
          "text": "Pessimistic for high contention, optimistic for low contention.\nAlways name **deadlock** as the risk."
        }
      ],
      "hero": "postgresql",
      "subtitle": "Concurrent writers"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "5. Isolation Levels",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "**What it is:** a setting that decides how much of other transactions' ==concurrent work== a transaction can see.",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "**Why it exists:** full isolation is slow, so databases let you trade ==correctness== for concurrency.",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "**Used for:** tuning each workload, such as a reporting query versus a payment write.",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "HOW IT WORKS",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "__Dirty read__: you see another transaction's uncommitted data",
            "__Non-repeatable read__: a row changes between two reads",
            "__Phantom read__: new rows appear in a repeated range query"
          ],
          "style": "dot",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Level",
            "Blocks",
            "Default in"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Read Uncommitted",
              "nothing",
              "none"
            ],
            [
              "Read Committed",
              "dirty reads",
              "PostgreSQL, Oracle"
            ],
            [
              "Repeatable Read",
              "+ non-repeatable",
              "MySQL InnoDB"
            ],
            [
              "Serializable",
              "all anomalies",
              "one by one"
            ]
          ],
          "frame": "dashed"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Step",
            "Read Committed",
            "Repeatable Read"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "T1 reads balance",
              "500",
              "500"
            ],
            [
              "T2 sets 400, commits",
              "400",
              "400"
            ],
            [
              "T1 rereads balance",
              "400",
              "500"
            ]
          ],
          "highlightRow": 2,
          "frame": "dashed"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "BEGIN;",
            "SET TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL",
            "  SERIALIZABLE;",
            "-- your statements here",
            "COMMIT;"
          ],
          "note": "set per transaction, right after BEGIN"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "BENEFITS",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Pick the ==cheapest level== that is still correct",
            "Reports run fast on Read Committed",
            "Payment writes get Serializable safety"
          ],
          "style": "check",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "THINGS TO CONSIDER",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Serializable can abort with serialization failures; the app must **retry**",
            "Repeatable Read can still permit ==write skew==",
            "Write skew: two doctors both go off-call"
          ],
          "style": "cross",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "INTERVIEW TAKEAWAY",
          "text": "Recite the four levels with the anomaly each one blocks.\nThen state the default of the database in question."
        }
      ],
      "hero": "postgresql",
      "subtitle": "how much you can see"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "7. MVCC",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. What it is",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "==Multi-Version Concurrency Control== keeps several versions of a row, and each transaction reads a __consistent snapshot__.",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Why it exists",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "With plain locks, **readers block writers** and writers block readers. MVCC removes that wait.",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Used for",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "{icon:postgresql} **PostgreSQL**, {icon:mysql} MySQL InnoDB and Oracle",
            "Implements Read Committed and Repeatable Read",
            "Done ==without read locks=="
          ],
          "style": "star",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. How it works",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Each row version has a creator ID `xmin`",
            "A deleter ID `xmax` marks it replaced",
            "A snapshot sees only versions ==committed before it began=="
          ],
          "style": "dot",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Example : row `price = 10` (xmin=100). At txn 120, `UPDATE` to 12 writes a **new version** and sets the old one xmax=120.",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "pink"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "tables",
          "spec": {
            "tables": [
              {
                "title": "Row versions of price",
                "headers": [
                  "price",
                  "xmin",
                  "xmax"
                ],
                "rows": [
                  [
                    "10",
                    "100",
                    "120"
                  ],
                  [
                    "12",
                    "120",
                    "-"
                  ]
                ]
              },
              {
                "title": "Who reads what",
                "headers": [
                  "Snapshot",
                  "Reads"
                ],
                "rows": [
                  [
                    "txn 110",
                    "10"
                  ],
                  [
                    "after 120 commits",
                    "12"
                  ]
                ]
              }
            ],
            "arrow": false
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "sticky",
          "title": "Old reader",
          "text": "Txn 110 still reads 10, even after the UPDATE."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Benefits vs Considerations",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Readers never block writers, writers never block readers",
            "Throughput stays high",
            "Snapshot reads give stable view for long reports"
          ],
          "style": "check",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Old versions pile up (bloat): PostgreSQL needs `VACUUM`, InnoDB needs undo purge",
            "Writers on the same row still **conflict**",
            "Long transactions block old-version cleanup"
          ],
          "style": "cross",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "INTERVIEW TAKEAWAY",
          "text": "\"Readers don't block writers because each reads its own snapshot.\" The cost is vacuum and storage."
        }
      ],
      "hero": "postgresql",
      "subtitle": "Snapshots, not locks"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "7. Connection Pooling",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "**What it is:** a ==cache of open database connections== that requests borrow and return, instead of opening a new one each time.",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "**Why it exists:** a new connection costs a TCP and TLS handshake plus auth, about ==20-50 ms==. PostgreSQL also spends roughly 10 MB of RAM per connection.",
          "frame": "none"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "**Used for:** web servers and serverless functions making many short queries.",
          "frame": "none"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "HOW IT WORKS",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Setup",
            "What happens",
            "Result"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "No pool",
              "500 req/s vs max_connections = 100",
              "connections exhausted, **errors**"
            ],
            [
              "Pool of 20",
              "borrow, run ~5 ms, return",
              "all 500 req/s served"
            ],
            [
              "Pool math",
              "20 conns x 200 queries/s each",
              "about 4,000 req/s capacity"
            ]
          ],
          "highlightRow": 1,
          "frame": "dashed"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "properties",
          "lines": [
            "# HikariCP settings",
            "maximumPoolSize=20",
            "minimumIdle=5",
            "connectionTimeout=3000",
            "idleTimeout=600000"
          ],
          "note": "connectionTimeout is the 3 s acquire timeout"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Tools: HikariCP (in app), PgBouncer, RDS Proxy",
            "Sizing rule: (CPU cores x 2) + effective disk spindles",
            "4 cores, 1 disk gives about ==9== connections",
            "**Bigger** is not faster: extra connections add contention"
          ],
          "style": "dot"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "BENEFITS",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Removes handshake latency from every request",
            "Caps database load, so connection storms can't hit it"
          ],
          "style": "check",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "THINGS TO CONSIDER",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Pool exhaustion: leaks or long transactions make requests queue, then time out",
            "PgBouncer transaction mode breaks session state: prepared statements, temp tables"
          ],
          "style": "cross",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "java",
          "lines": [
            "Connection c = pool.getConnection();",
            "try {",
            "  run(c, sql);",
            "} finally {",
            "  c.close(); // returns to pool",
            "}"
          ],
          "note": "close() returns it, never leaks"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "INTERVIEW TAKEAWAY",
          "text": "A small pool in front of the database beats many connections. Always release connections in a finally block."
        }
      ],
      "hero": "postgresql",
      "subtitle": "Borrow, don't reopen"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "9. Partitioning",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "**What it is:** one big table split into smaller ==partitions== in the same server, by a partition key",
            "**Why it exists:** giant tables make queries, index upkeep and deletes slow",
            "**Used for:** time-series logs, orders by month, data retention"
          ],
          "style": "dot",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "HOW IT WORKS",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "==Range==: by date, one partition per month",
            "==List==: by region, e.g. EU, US, APAC",
            "==Hash==: `user_id` modulo N, spreads rows evenly"
          ],
          "style": "star"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Example : hash on 4 partitions, `user_id` 10 gives 10 % 4 = 2, so the row lands in partition p2.",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "pink"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "CREATE TABLE events (",
            "  id bigint, ts date, ...",
            ") PARTITION BY RANGE (ts);",
            "-- partitions: events_2026_09",
            "-- and events_2026_10"
          ],
          "note": "the key goes in PARTITION BY"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Query",
            "What happens"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`WHERE ts = '2026-10-06'`",
              "reads events_2026_10 only"
            ],
            [
              "Partitions never read",
              "**11** of 12 monthly ones"
            ],
            [
              "Drop old data",
              "`DROP TABLE events_2025_01`"
            ],
            [
              "Same job with DELETE",
              "huge, slow; DROP is instant"
            ]
          ],
          "highlightRow": 0,
          "frame": "dashed"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Tip",
          "text": "Run `EXPLAIN` and check the plan lists only one partition."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "BENEFITS",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "__Partition pruning__ skips irrelevant data, so queries run faster",
            "Smaller per-partition indexes stay shallow and quick",
            "Cheap archival: detach or drop a whole partition",
            "Each partition is vacuumed or reindexed separately"
          ],
          "style": "check"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "THINGS TO CONSIDER",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Queries that omit the key **scan every partition**",
            "A skewed key makes uneven, lopsided partitions",
            "Unique constraints must include the partition key"
          ],
          "style": "cross"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "INTERVIEW TAKEAWAY",
          "text": "Partitioning is one machine; sharding is many machines.\nPick a partition key you filter on often."
        }
      ],
      "hero": "postgresql",
      "subtitle": "One table, many slices"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "10. Replication",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "10. Replication",
          "ink": "accent"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "**What it is:** continuously copying data from a ==primary== node to one or more ==replicas== on other servers",
            "**Why it exists:** fault tolerance, read scaling, and lower latency with replicas placed near users",
            "**Used for:** failover, read-heavy apps, analytics replicas, and backups"
          ],
          "style": "dot",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "HOW IT WORKS",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "__Single-leader:__ writes go to the primary, reads go to replicas",
            "Multi-leader and leaderless (Dynamo-style) designs also exist",
            "{blue:Sync} waits for the replica before ack: no data loss, higher latency",
            "{orange:Async} acks at once: fast, but replicas lag"
          ],
          "style": "dot",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flowchart",
          "spec": {
            "title": "Primary in us-east-1, 2 replicas",
            "rows": [
              [
                "app"
              ],
              [
                "primary"
              ],
              [
                "r1",
                "r2"
              ]
            ],
            "nodes": {
              "app": {
                "text": "App writes",
                "shape": "pill",
                "ink": "blue"
              },
              "primary": {
                "text": "Primary us-east-1",
                "shape": "icon",
                "icon": "postgresql",
                "ink": "green"
              },
              "r1": {
                "text": "Replica 1",
                "shape": "icon",
                "icon": "postgresql",
                "ink": "blue"
              },
              "r2": {
                "text": "Replica 2 (300 ms behind)",
                "shape": "icon",
                "icon": "postgresql",
                "ink": "orange"
              }
            },
            "edges": [
              {
                "from": "app",
                "to": "primary",
                "label": "write"
              },
              {
                "from": "primary",
                "to": "r1",
                "label": "copy"
              },
              {
                "from": "primary",
                "to": "r2",
                "label": "async copy"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Time",
            "Action",
            "User sees"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "t=0",
              "Write name='Ana' to primary",
              "ack"
            ],
            [
              "t=100 ms",
              "Read from replica 2",
              "old name"
            ],
            [
              "t=300 ms",
              "Replica 2 catches up",
              "'Ana'"
            ]
          ],
          "highlightRow": 1,
          "frame": "dashed"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "sticky",
          "title": "Fix: read-your-writes",
          "text": "For a few seconds after a write, route that user's reads to the **primary**."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "BENEFITS vs CONSIDER",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "{icon:postgresql} Primary dies: a replica is ==promoted==, system stays up (failover)",
            "Reads scale **horizontally** by adding replicas"
          ],
          "style": "check",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Replication lag causes **stale reads**",
            "Async failover can lose recent writes",
            "Writes do not scale: all hit one primary",
            "__Split-brain__ risk if two nodes both lead"
          ],
          "style": "cross",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "INTERVIEW TAKEAWAY",
          "text": "Replication scales ==reads== and availability, not writes.\nName the sync vs async trade-off and the lag problem."
        }
      ],
      "hero": "postgresql",
      "subtitle": "Copies for uptime"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "11. Sharding",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "11. Sharding",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "**What it is:** ==horizontally splitting== data across independent database servers (shards), each holding a subset of rows."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "**Why it exists:** one server runs out of disk, memory or write throughput, and ==replication== cannot fix write limits."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "**Used for:** very large systems: social feeds, chat, and multi-tenant SaaS."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "HOW IT WORKS",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "==Range-based==: user_id 1-1M on shard A",
            "==Hash-based==: hash(user_id) mod N",
            "==Directory-based==: a lookup table maps key to shard"
          ],
          "style": "dot",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "tables",
          "spec": {
            "tables": [
              {
                "title": "shard = user_id % 4",
                "headers": [
                  "user_id",
                  "calc",
                  "shard"
                ],
                "rows": [
                  [
                    "10",
                    "10 % 4",
                    "2"
                  ],
                  [
                    "13",
                    "13 % 4",
                    "1"
                  ],
                  [
                    "8",
                    "8 % 4",
                    "0"
                  ]
                ],
                "caption": "4 shards, plain modulo"
              }
            ],
            "arrow": false
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Adding a 5th shard with plain modulo remaps **most** keys. ==Consistent hashing== moves only about 1/N of them.",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Strategy",
            "Good for",
            "Risk"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Range",
              "range scans",
              "hot newest shard"
            ],
            [
              "Hash",
              "even spread",
              "hard range scans"
            ],
            [
              "Directory",
              "flexible moves",
              "lookup is a bottleneck"
            ]
          ],
          "frame": "dashed"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "def shard(user_id):",
            "  return user_id % 4",
            "# shard(10) -> 2, shard(13) -> 1"
          ],
          "note": "same key always hits same shard"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "cards",
          "spec": {
            "cards": [
              {
                "title": "Benefits",
                "lines": [
                  "✓ Writes and storage scale almost linearly with shards",
                  "✓ Failures and load stay isolated to one shard"
                ],
                "ink": "green"
              },
              {
                "title": "Things to consider",
                "lines": [
                  "⚠ Cross-shard joins and transactions are costly, or need two-phase commit",
                  "⚠ Hot shards (a celebrity user) and costly resharding",
                  "⚠ Pick a high-cardinality, evenly distributed key"
                ],
                "ink": "red"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Tip",
          "text": "Put a tenant or user ID in every query, so it routes to one shard."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "INTERVIEW TAKEAWAY",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Shard last",
          "text": "Shard only after indexing, caching, replication and partitioning are exhausted. Then justify the shard key."
        }
      ],
      "hero": "database",
      "subtitle": "Split data across servers"
    },
